  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Quiet and rather benign conditions will\ncontinue through the next several days as a dry west to southwest\nflow continues over the forecast area through the short term. This\nwill result in fairly pleasant fall-like conditions for the rest\nof the week with highs in the 70s to mid 80s and lows in the 30s \nand 40s. Winds will be on the breezy side Thursday and Friday and \nthese will come into play concerning areas of elevated fire \nweather concerns. These will be handled in the fire weather \nsection below. Wind speeds will generally run 10 to 20 mph with \ngusts to 30 mph on Thursday and slightly stronger for Friday with \nspeeds and gusts some 5 to 10 mph higher.\n\nModels are depicting an upper level trough setting up along the\nwestern Pacific for the weekend. The dry southwest flow is\nexpected to continue through the weekend with low end\nprobabilities for light precipitation. Heights will gradually\ndiminish and as a result, afternoon high temperatures will also\ncool several degrees during the weekend. Readings will be in the\nlow 70s to low 80s for high temperatures with lows in the 30s and\n40s for overnight low temperatures. Winds will be breezy across \ncentral Nevada. \n\nThe upper trough may be pushing across the region during the early\nto middle portions of next week. Continued cool conditions with\nbreezy winds and light precipitation can be expected.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Adjusted wind and wind gust\ngrids for the latter portions of the week as the upper trough\ncomes ashore across the Pacific northwest. This was the only\nchange made to the NBM forecast.\n\nConfidence continues to remain low in the weak storm system for \nthe early to mid-week portion. There is a fifteen to twenty-five \npercent probability for isolated showers across northern Nevada \nthrough Monday of next week. There is low confidence for upper \nlevel ridging during the middle part of next week.",
